{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"═══ EPIC 061c IS CLOSED. 8/8 ACs. Blocker 129e closed, story 8625 closed, all 18 children closed. ═══\n\n## FINAL STATE\n  061c  closed  8/8 ACs   LLM provider seam\n  129e  closed  7/7 ACs   --class regression, change 1344 MERGED 929ba5d669\n  8625  closed  4/4 ACs   change 1350 MERGED 996a3b107f\n  846b  OPEN    (new)     the gate gap; immediate blockage resolved by 1355, general case not\n  All 18 children closed. Store synced: unpushed 0, every write an ancestor of origin/tickets.\n\n## MERGED THIS SESSION\n  1350  996a3b107f  8625: correct the per-class model env examples\n  1355  75cdfec3572 846b: pin the maintainer gate environment\n  1344  929ba5d669  129e: guarantee T1 web access on every provider\n\n## THE THREE THINGS THAT MATTERED\n\n### 1. 129e's Verified -1 was NOT the stale-base trap the handoff predicted\nAll four CI legs failed identically on two tests from my own new file. Genuine host-dependence:\nthe bedrock leg of a provider-parametrized test builds a REAL provider (only the Agent is doubled),\nand `build_bedrock_provider` resolves BOTH a region AND credentials at construction. This host had\nboth; CI had neither. The previously-cited \"9863 passed\" was green only because of ambient host AWS\nconfig. TWO arms, and the first fix proved one was not enough: no region -> LLMConfigError from the\nboto3 pre-check; region pinned but no creds -> botocore reaches IMDS and trips the\nnetwork-forbidden fixture. `REBAR_LLM_BEDROCK_REGION` does NOT help — it is read only by\nLLMConfig's env/table factory, never by direct construction; pin the FIELD. Fixed test-only,\nverified stripped-env AND on-host (20 passed both). CI then went Verified +1.\n\n### 2. A gate that landed the day before was unsatisfiable from any developer environment (846b)\n`bugfix_size_gate.py` blocks a >150-non-test-line bug fix unless the bug carries a plan-review\nop-cert whose principal is PINNED in `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`. Only `nava-opcert-prod`\nwas pinned; its key is SSM-only, the service holding it has NO client in this repo, and by design\n(`REBAR_SYNC_PUSH: \"off\"`) it never persists what it signs. The keyring check runs BEFORE any drift\nclassification, so a local signature can never reach the ACCEPTED `stale-code`/`stale-head` arms —\nit short-circuits to `mismatch` and blocks unconditionally.\nNO PRECEDENT: the only recent over-floor bug fix, e3cd (229 non-test lines), merged 2026-08-02, ONE\nDAY BEFORE the gate, with no attestation at all. 129e was the first change to meet it.\nOperator chose to pin this environment. Era start = the tickets tip at pinning, NOT backdated, so\n129e's existing cert fell outside the era and had to be re-signed (free — `sign-review` replays the\nsidecar). VERIFIED BY RUNNING THE GATE'S OWN CLASSIFIER before spending a CI cycle:\n`classify_plan_review_attestation(...)` went from `mismatch` (blocks) to `stale-head`, whose\n`bucket_for_verdict` is `accepted`. LLM-Review then went +1 on the next push, as predicted.\n\n### 3. The epic's own plan carried a FALSE claim, and the review caught it\nFirst epic review: BLOCK, G6, critical. The plan presented a botocore retry envelope\n(`Config(retries=…, mode=\"adaptive\")` via `BedrockProvider(bedrock_client=…)` plus\n`aws_read_timeout`) as ACHIEVED and concluded retry/timeout parity \"is the parity S6 measures\".\nNone of it ships — `bedrock_model.py:101` is a bare `BedrockProvider(region_name=region)`, so\n`llm_retry_max_attempts`/`llm_retry_max_wait_s`/`timeout_s` never reach Bedrock, and production IS\nBedrock, so the unconfigured path is the live one. The already-merged ADR 0059 and\ndocs/llm-framework.md both already admitted this; only the epic's plan still claimed otherwise. The\nreviewer also corrected the mechanism (`bedrock_client=`, not the `aws_read_timeout` kwarg).\nAccepted rather than argued: description corrected to state the gap and defer to 61d8. Second run\nPASS, 0 blocking, SIGNED. Two paid reviews, and the first paid for itself.\n\n## A DOC DEFECT ONLY EXECUTION COULD FIND (8625 AC2)\nAll three `REBAR_LLM_<CLASS>_MODEL` examples were paired with `rebar review <id>` — which declares\nNO model class, so it resolves the top-level `model` and the per-class vars do nothing to it. I got\nthe diagnosis wrong TWICE and a control corrected me each time:\n 1. Assumed the top-level pin was SHADOWING the class. A scratch repo with no top-level model still\n    resolved the DEFAULT, not gpt-4o — the class is not shadowed, a classless op never consults it.\n 2. A narrow grep then said `frontier` had NO consumer at all. Searching ALL FILE TYPES found\n    `gates/code-review.yaml:74  model: frontier`. Live via workflow STEP declarations, not a Python\n    constant. (Third time this repo has punished a narrow grep — the lesson keeps holding.)\nProof the correction is right, live: `REBAR_VERIFY_ENABLE_CODE_REVIEW=1\nREBAR_LLM_FRONTIER_MODEL=openai:gpt-4o rebar review-code` routed\n`[code-review-base] model=openai:gpt-4o` and REACHED OpenAI, failing only on\n`credit_balance_exhausted`. Routing proven; the account has no credits.\n\n## 129e AC1/AC2 SETTLED BY LIVE MEASUREMENT against the merged code\n  Bedrock  us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8: supported_native_tools = frozenset()  -> route \"local\"\n  Anthropic claude-opus-4-8: supported_native_tools ∋ WebSearchTool            -> route \"native\"\n  web_search_capabilities(web=True)[0] is a WebSearch with get_toolset() is not None (the trap\n  guard holds); web=False -> None.\nSo Bedrock does NOT carry the server-side tool and T1 is served there by the local route — both\nderived from the profile registry, never a name. The per-provider runner cases now pass in CI on\nall four matrix legs, i.e. with no AWS config and no provider credentials.\n\n## EPIC AC EVIDENCE — where each ended up\n  AC1 all 18 closed; eb58 has ZERO commits and is a DECISION story (code landed under 3768/b690) —\n      ticked with that reasoning written INTO the criterion, not silently.\n  AC2 was MISFILED: verify-completion-on-Bedrock lived on 1aa2, not 2932. Consolidated onto 2932.\n  AC4 NEWLY PRODUCED against a live OpenAI-compatible endpoint: isinstance(OpenAIProvider) True,\n      base_url = the local endpoint, tier best_effort, and a REAL served request. Also reached the\n      LMStudio on this host's :1234 (a genuine third-party server), proving routing independently.\n  AC5 already on 2932 (cache_write=4017 then cache_read=4017) — NOT re-run.\n  AC6 demonstrated on the epic's own signed verdict (provider/endpoint_host/tier populated).\n  AC7 1344's code-review sidecar: provider=bedrock, model=…opus-4-8, first_class.\n  AC8 already on eb6e (alarm ARN, config read back from CloudWatch, forced notification delivered).\n\n## CORRECTIONS TO THE PRIOR HANDOFF\n* 129e is NOT a child of the epic — `parent_id` null, it `blocks 061c`. Closing it emitted\n  `UNBLOCKED: 061c`. (The handoff's own warning about outgoing-only edges applied to itself.)\n* \"Try sign-review FIRST, it will clear the block\" was only half right: sign-review DID re-sign for\n  free, but local signing can NEVER satisfy that gate — it moved the error from \"unsigned\" to\n  \"mismatch\". The pin was required.\n* The tickets branch is healthy. The 108-commit backlog is gone; what remains is ordinary busy-ref\n  contention (union-merge + retry, 1-2 attempts). fsck reports `diverged` with \"no shared history\",\n  which is a MIS-DIAGNOSIS — there was a shared merge-base every time.\n\n## MISTAKES I MADE, recorded\n* Ran `rebar review-code`/`transition` INSIDE a worktree whose full suite was running. The suite\n  reported 4 errors — \"Test leaked new entries into REPO_ROOT\" — on four unrelated tests, caused by\n  my own concurrent writes, not the change (one YAML file). Clean re-run: 9901 passed, 0 errors.\n  RULE: never run a store or gate command in a worktree whose suite is in flight.\n* Wrote three assertions in the AC4 probe that were too strict (exact class name, hardcoded port)\n  and had to relax each. The provider is `_RebarOpenAICompatibleProvider`, a SUBCLASS of\n  OpenAIProvider — assert isinstance, not the name.\n\n## GOTCHAS CONFIRMED / NEW\n* `uv pip install '.[a,b]'` does NOT honour `[tool.uv] conflicts` (exit 0); only `uv sync` does\n  (exit 2). The doc's \"not co-installable\" is true of the sync/lock path.\n* `rebar audit show` text output is NOT valid JSON — parse as text.\n* Closing a bug REQUIRES `--class`; the error names the nine values.\n* `rebar transition` on an epic can exceed 600s (completion verifier) — the status change COMMITS\n  before the trailing work, so a SIGTERM at 10m can still leave it correctly closed. Verify status\n  rather than assuming failure.\n* `rebar review-plan` on a bug can take the fast structural path (`claim_path: no-llm/no-network`),\n  in which case T1 never runs and `web_access` is honestly \"off\". Not evidence of a missing fix."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785863154530638001,"uuid":"a8ad9a22-7476-43a6-8e6c-6d6ecefafc59"}